===Etymology===
From {{inh|ja|ojp|-}}, from {{inh|ja|jpx-pro|*iku}}. Cognate with {{cog|ryu|-}} {{ryu-r|行ちゅん|いちゅん}}, {{cog|mvi|行きぃ|tr=iksï}}.

The original Proto‐Japonic root was vowel‐initial, as evidenced by the Ryukyuan cognates. In Old Japanese, a prothetic glide {{IPAfont|/j/}} was often inserted before certain vowel‐initial words to ease pronunciation. This phonological innovation created the variant form {{ja-r|行く|ゆく}}, paralleling the ''i‐'' ~ ''yu‐'' alternation seen in the archaic noun {{m|ojp|夢|tr=ime<sub>2</sub>}} versus modern {{ja-r|夢|ゆめ}}.

Because the innovative ''yuku'' form was heavily utilized in foundational classical literature, such as the ''[[w:Man'yōshū|Man’yōshū]]'', it became fossilized as the high‐prestige, conservative literary standard. Meanwhile, the direct descendant ''iku'' persisted as the common spoken vernacular and was ultimately adopted as the standard for modern spoken Japanese.

