====Usage notes====
* In English, the verbs {{m|en|go}} and {{m|en|come}} in the senses of physical motion are often used in relation to the listener.  If Alice calls Bob and asks him if he is on his way to her party, he would say, "I'm coming."
: However, in Japanese, the verbs {{ja-r|行く|いく|to [[go]]}} and {{ja-r|来る|くる|to [[come]]}} are used in relation to the speaker.  If Alice calls Bob and asks him if he is on his way to her party, he would instead say {{m|ja|行きます|tr=ikimasu||I'm going}}.
* This verb has an irregular [[w:Onbin|euphonic stem]] in its perfective and conjunctive constructions, those being {{ja-r|行った|いった}} and {{ja-r|行って|いって}} respectively, instead of the expected {{ja-r|行いた|い.いた|linkto=-}} and {{ja-r|行いて|い.いて|linkto=-}} or {{ja-r|行いた|ゆいた|linkto=-}} and {{ja-r|行いて|ゆいて|linkto=-}}.
* As an auxiliary, it is more commonly spelled in hiragana. The form {{ja-r|-[[て]] いく}} can also be colloquially shortened to {{ja-r|てく|-てく}}.
* While generally written as {{ja-l|行}}, this verb can be written with several other kanji to convey specific nuances. For instance, {{ja-l|往}} emphasizes proceeding towards a destination or the passage of time, while {{ja-l|逝}} is a euphemism meaning ‘to pass away’. Other rare, historical, or literary spellings include {{ja-l|適}}, {{ja-l|之}}, and {{ja-l|征}} (the latter typically implying a military campaign or expedition).

